{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ police killed unarmed people statistics database)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1461561434#8_3028732033","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1448","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1461561434#8_3028732033\" , \"end_char\": 6526.000000 , \"headings\": \"\\\"Last year, there were nine unarmed black people killed (by law enforcement). Nineteen unarmed white people.” \\\"Last year, there were nine unarmed black people <cover>killed (by law enforcement). Nineteen unarmed white people.” Larry Elder mislabels statistics on fatal shootings by police If Your Time is short The numbers Featured Fact-check California Gov. Gavin Newsom “just let 76,000 inmates out of jail.\" What’s considered armed? Missing context Our ruling Support independent fact-checking. Become a member! \" , \"segment\": \"The Washington Post’s database </cover>doesn’t include those people in its unarmed tallies. In 2019, at least eight black people and 16 white people were killed by police while in possession of a toy weapon or something similar, according to Mapping Police Violence. There are also numerous 2019 cases, in both data sets, where the victim’s armed status remains unknown or the victim was considered armed with a vehicle. Overall, the data on whether police-killing victims were armed or unarmed is \""}}
