{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1575802734#0_2909135761","relevance":3,"reason":"This segment directly answers frequency with counts and a rate: police killed 776 people so far in 2015, 161 completely unarmed, averaging about three people per day, based on The Guardian's The Counted database.","learned":"The Guardian's The Counted reported an average of about three police killings per day in 2015 to that point, with 161 unarmed victims among 776 total deaths."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1448","reason":"The current query is still yielding highly relevant database-based statistics on unarmed people killed by police, so continue to collect more grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1448"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1461561434#4_3028722951","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1448","rank":4,"snippet":"Prevention and FBI keep tabs on deaths resulting from interactions with police, but both sources provide incomplete data that takes years to record. More reliable data comes from the Washington Post and projects such as Mapping Police Violence and Fatal Encounters. In emails to PolitiFact, Elder cited a tweet and a Wall Street Journal op-ed that pulled numbers from the Washington Post’s <cover>database. According to the Washington Post data through June 3, police in the line of duty fatally shot 10 unarmed black people and 20 unarmed white people in 2019. ( The data is updated as new facts emerge, and by June 5 the numbers jumped to 15 unarmed blacks and 25 unarmed whites.) But Elder incorrectly claimed that his statistics represented the numbers of all black and white Americans who were killed </cover>by police, not the number of \"shootings in which a police officer, in the line of duty, shoots and kills a civilian,\" as the Washington Post describes its data. The Washington Post doesn’t account for deaths like Floyd’s, for example. Floyd died in police custody May 25 after an officer in Minneapolis kneeled on his neck.\" , \"start_char\":"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"9/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1461561434#4_3028722951","relevance":3,"reason":"This fact-check segment directly addresses counts of unarmed people fatally shot by police, citing Washington Post data through June 2019 and explaining the scope and limitations of the database versus all police-caused deaths.","learned":"Washington Post data through June 3, 2019 listed 10 unarmed Black people and 20 unarmed white people fatally shot by on-duty police; by June 5 the updated counts were 15 and 25, but the database excludes non-shooting custody deaths like George Floyd."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1448","reason":"The result list is still producing directly relevant database-based statistics and fact-checking about unarmed victims killed by police, so continue."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1448"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1461561434#9_3028734603","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1448","rank":5,"snippet":"including force that could be deadly but doesn’t turn out to be fatal. Missing context Elder’s claim also ignores the fact that the same Washington Post data shows black people are disproportionately shot and killed by police, experts said. \"There may be more unarmed whites than unarmed blacks killed each year, but the rate of shooting unarmed blacks is much larger,\" Fridell said, citing a report from the Washington Post that summarized years of findings from its <cover>database. Black Americans represent 13% of the U.S. population, the report said, but they accounted for about a quarter of police shooting victims over four and a half years of data collection. \"They're over-represented in the data,\" said Brian Burghart, the founder of Fatal Encounters. Our ruling Elder said: \" Last year, there were nine unarmed black people killed (by law enforcement). Nineteen unarmed white people.\"\" , \"start_char\": 5763.000000 , \"title\": \"PolitiFact | Larry Elder mislabels statistics </cover>on fatal shootings by police\" , \"url\": \"https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/jun/05/larry-elder/larry-elder-mislabels-statistics-fatal-shootings-p/\" }"}}
