{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_608629529#6_680849525","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage gives direct statistics on innocent people/bystanders killed during police no-knock or quick-knock raids, including raid frequency and a Cato Institute count of forty bystanders killed since the early 1980s. This is a strong literal subset of innocent victims killed by officers.","learned":"No-knock/quick-knock raids were estimated at 60,000–70,000 annually around 2010, and the Cato Institute reported forty bystanders killed in such raids since the early 1980s."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1453","reason":"This query is finding literal innocent-bystander police-killing statistics, so continue to seek more direct evidence on bystanders or innocent victims killed by officers."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1453"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_611887242#0_1369447483","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1453","rank":2,"snippet":"Ph.D. “Perhaps the most compelling, ongoing, and logical reason for law enforcement’s continued interest in high-speed vehicle pursuits has been its concern in balancing the values of crime control and offender apprehension with ensuring the safety of all parties who potentially might be involved—police officers, suspects, victims, <cover>bystanders, and the community.” 1 This balancing test has formed the cornerstone of pursuit policies, training, and practice for the past several decades. Police pursuit records provide some frightening statistics. First, the majority of police pursuits involve a stop for a traffic violation. Second, one person dies every day as a result of a police pursuit. On average, from 1994 through 1998, one law enforcement officer was killed every 11 weeks in a pursuit, and 1 percent of all U.S. law enforcement officers who died in the line of duty lost their lives in vehicle pursuits. Innocent </cover>third parties who just happened to be in the way constitute 42 percent of persons killed or injured in police pursuits. Further, 1 out of every 100 high-speed pursuits results in a fatality. 2 Mr. Schultz works with the Minnesota Highway Safety and Research Center in St. Cloud.\" ,"}}
