{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"police shootings\" \"unarmed\" \"declined\" \"2015\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_595588054#5_846171482","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1459","rank":3,"snippet":"shot by police are white. Most of these shootings draw little or no attention beyond a news story. Advertisement Some become flash points in the country’s ongoing reckoning about race and police. The ones prompting the loudest outcries often involve people who are black, unarmed, or both, shootings that have led to the harshest scrutiny of police. Since The Post began tracking the shootings, black people have been shot and killed by police at disproportionate rates — both in terms of overall shootings and the shootings of unarmed Americans. The number of black and <cover>unarmed people fatally shot by police has declined since 2015, but whether armed or not, black people are still shot and killed at a disproportionately higher rate than white people.\" , \"start_char\": 4552.000000 , \"title\": \"Police in the U.S. have shot and killed more than 5,000 people since 2015 - The Washington Post\" , \"url\": \"https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/protests-spread-over-police-shootings-</cover>police-promised-reforms-every-year-they-still-shoot-nearly-1000-people/2020/06/08/5c204f0c-a67c-11ea-b473-04905b1af82b_story.html\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_592642848#3_840690344","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_591732046#0_839444811","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
