{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_595588054#5_846171482","relevance":3,"reason":"The Washington Post article is dedicated to police shootings and directly supports frequency evidence: police in the U.S. have shot and killed more than 5,000 people since 2015, nearly 1,000 per year, with discussion of unarmed victims and racial disproportionality.","learned":"Washington Post reported that U.S. police have shot and killed more than 5,000 people since 2015 and still shoot nearly 1,000 people each year; unarmed fatal shootings have declined since 2015 but remain racially disproportionate."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_595588054#5_846171482","reason":"Open this highly relevant Washington Post article to capture fuller exact frequency figures, trend statements, and unarmed-victim evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_595588054#5_846171482"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_595588054#5_846171482","document":"Police in the U.S. have shot and killed more than 5,000 people since 2015 - The Washington Post ... Protests spread over police shootings. Police promised reforms. Every year, they still shoot and kill nearly 1,000 people. Protests spread over police shootings. Police promised reforms. Every year, they still shoot and kill nearly 1,000 people. 'The past-due notice for unpaid debts' 'This is without question a murder' 'No national standards' No nationwide data on use of force America’s Racial Reckoning: What you need to know  ... “It is difficult to explain why we haven’t seen significant fluctuations in the shooting from year to year,” former Charlotte police chief Darrel Stephens said. “ There’s been significant investments that have been made in de-escalation training. There’s been a lot of work.” Story continues below advertisement The overwhelming majority of people killed are armed. Nearly half of all people fatally shot by police are white. Most of these shootings draw little or no attention beyond a news story. Advertisement Some become flash points in the country’s ongoing reckoning about race and police. The ones prompting the loudest outcries often involve people who are black, unarmed, or both, shootings that have led to the harshest scrutiny of police. Since The Post began tracking the shootings, black people have been shot and killed by police at disproportionate rates — both in terms of overall shootings and the shootings of unarmed Americans. The number of black and unarmed people fatally shot by police has declined since 2015, but whether armed or not, black people are still shot and killed at a disproportionately higher rate than white people.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
