{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_482627231#0_843961624","relevance":1,"reason":"The full document is related to police brutality and mentions claims about unarmed Black men being killed by police, but the available text does not provide an exact count or frequency of innocent/unarmed victims killed by officers.","learned":"Some polemical police-brutality statistics pages discuss unarmed victims but may not contain usable counts in the accessible text."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"police pursuits\" \"innocent\" \"killed\" statistics)","reason":"Search for additional literal innocent-victim statistics in police pursuits, a productive direction that previously yielded direct evidence about innocent third parties killed or injured."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"police pursuits\" \"innocent\" \"killed\" statistics)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_1315850027#5_2333029583","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1465","rank":1,"snippet":"Death About the author \" , \"segment\": \"The Portland Police Bureau shared additional research in 2015 that illustrated how secondary impacts were more common in target vehicles equipped with ESC than without. Additionally, the severity of secondary impacts increased at speeds higher than 45 mph. Injury and Death The officer and citizen injuries and deaths resulting from high-speed police pursuits are shockingly high. <cover>Innocent third-party observers who just happened to be in the way add up to 42 percent of persons killed or injured during pursuits. USA Today reports that more than 5,000 bystander and passenger deaths have occurred as a result of police pursuits since 1979, and other research shows that roughly 40 percent of police pursuits result in crashes. With these statistics, </cover>as well as modern vehicle safety features like electronic stability control, executing a PIT maneuver has become more complicated for law enforcement. Continued research of the PIT on newer vehicles will be necessary to ensure that the technique remains effective, and officers will need to train thoroughly so they can effectively execute the PIT without incident. During a pursuit, officers are charged with the responsibility of protecting the public "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"30/50"}}
