{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"bystander\" deaths \"police pursuits\" \"since 1979\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_1315850027#4_2333027549","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1466","rank":1,"snippet":"Researchers observed in 2008 that the PIT maneuver may be less effective on vehicles equipped with ESC. Further data illustrated additional necessary steering input, modified acceleration and braking to successfully execute the PIT on a target vehicle equipped with ESC. The Portland Police Bureau shared additional research in 2015 that illustrated how secondary impacts were more common in target vehicles equipped with ESC than without. Additionally, the severity of secondary impacts increased at speeds higher than 45 mph. Injury and Death The officer and citizen injuries and deaths resulting from high-speed police pursuits are shockingly high. Innocent third-party observers who just happened to be in the way add up to 42 percent of persons killed or injured during pursuits. USA Today reports that more than 5,000 <cover>bystander and passenger deaths have occurred as a result of police pursuits since 1979, </cover>and other research shows that roughly 40 percent of police pursuits result in crashes.\" , \"start_char\": 2573.000000 , \"title\": \"The limitations of the PIT maneuver in police pursuits\" , \"url\": \"https://pursuitresponse.org/limitations-pit-maneuver-police-pursuits/\" }"}}
